<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Use of Technology in School</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a recent personal experience. Interactive: Exchange two or more ideas in group discussions on familiar subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe and recommend an educational app during a group discussion based on a recent personal experience (e.g., "I used a language learning app for language practice, and it helped me learn new vocabulary easily.").
                Can exchange ideas in group discussions about useful technology in school by sharing personal preferences and comparing features (e.g., "I prefer tablets for reading because they are light and easy to use, but laptops are better for writing reports.").
                Can participate in discussions about technology limitations by suggesting solutions or alternatives (e.g., "We should limit technology use during lunch to talk more with friends. Instead, we can play outdoor games to stay active.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting images of different educational technologies (e.g., tablets, interactive whiteboards, educational apps). Students work in pairs and have 1 minute to share one benefit and one problem of using these technologies in school, using the vocabulary from previous lessons. For example, "I think tablets are useful, but they can be distracting."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models responses focusing on expressing opinions and comparing preferences. "I believe technology is beneficial because I can access many resources easily. However, we should be careful about screen time. For example, I prefer e-books on a tablet, but I think traditional books are better for focused reading." The teacher clearly articulates multisyllabic words like "beneficial" and "distracting," and models intonation for "should" and "can."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts orally.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'beneficial', is it a good thing or a bad thing?" (Expected: A good thing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'distracting', does it help you focus or make you lose focus?" (Expected: Makes you lose focus.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two 9th-grade students discussing their favorite educational apps, devices they use, and suggestions for appropriate technology use in school. The dialogue includes expressions of preference, suggestions, and descriptions of personal experiences. (Genre: Dialogue, Register: Conversational, Format: Audio recording).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand and engage in simple conversations about familiar topics related to technology in education, identifying opinions and specific details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (First listen):</b> "What are the two students mainly talking about?" (Expected: How they use technology in school, and what they think about it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second listen):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements are true or false (e.g., "Student A prefers laptops for all tasks." "Student B recommends a language learning app.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match each student's name to a specific opinion or suggestion (e.g., "Suggests limiting phone use at lunch." "Describes using an app for vocabulary progress.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align="justify"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using the simple present for expressing opinions and comparing preferences, modals "should" (suggestions) and "can" (ability), Wh-questions, and the target vocabulary (technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, prefer, limit, recommend, device, resources) with a focus on pronunciation of multisyllabic words and intonation of modals.</p>
                <p style="text-align="justify"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Builders (5 min):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on the board for students to complete orally in pairs, ensuring correct use of target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I think technology is ____ because ____." (e.g., "useful," "accessible")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I prefer ____ (device) for ____, but ____ (device) is better for ____."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We should ____ (verb) technology when ____." (e.g., "limit," "ignore")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ____ (verb) ____ (noun) with my computer." (e.g., "create presentations," "browse resources")</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students take turns saying their sentences, focusing on correct word order and pronunciation (e.g., stress in "information," "innovative").
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Tech Survey (6 min):</b> Students interview a partner using a short survey with Wh-questions provided by the teacher. They must ask and answer using full sentences, expressing preferences and reasons. The teacher reminds them to use discourse markers (while, but, instead, because) and checks for intonation patterns with "should" and "can."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What educational apps do you like the most? Why?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which device do you prefer for schoolwork: a tablet or a laptop? Why?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one problem with using technology in school, and what should we do about it?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Problem-Solution Brainstorm (4 min):</b> In pairs, students are given a specific "problem" related to technology in school (e.g., "Students get distracted by games during class time." "Some students don't have access to devices at home.") and brainstorm at least two "solutions." They must use "should" to suggest solutions and "can" to express what actions can be taken.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Problem: Too much screen time during breaks. Solution: We should play outdoor games instead of browsing on our phones. We can talk to friends face-to-face."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion: "Debating the Role of Technology in Our School." Students form groups of 3-4. Each group member will participate in a discussion where they need to:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe a recent personal experience using an educational app or device (e.g., "I used a language learning app for practice, and it helped me learn new vocabulary easily.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recommend an educational app or digital resource to their peers, explaining its benefits.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Express personal preferences for different technologies and compare their features (e.g., "I prefer tablets for reading because they are light, but laptops are better for writing reports.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Suggest solutions or limits for common problems related to technology use in school (e.g., "We should limit technology use during lunch to talk more with friends instead.").</li>
                </ul>
                Students are encouraged to use Wh-questions to engage their peers and the discourse markers (while, but, instead, because) to elaborate on their ideas. The teacher moves around, observing, listening for the correct use of target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation of multisyllabic words and modal intonation, offering support as needed without interrupting fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a checklist to monitor individual and group participation during the "Debating the Role of Technology in Our School" discussion, noting the use of specific language features.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' active participation in the group discussion, demonstrating their ability to speak, interact, and negotiate meaning using the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "education," "innovation," "software"), and appropriate intonation for "should" and "can."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, device, prefer, limit, recommend) in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Expressing opinions, comparisons, and suggestions clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct use of simple present for opinions/preferences, modals "should" and "can," and Wh-questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Exchanging ideas, listening to others, and responding appropriately.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket at the end of the lesson.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about discussing technology in English is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I want to improve in my speaking about technology is..."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about discussing the benefits and drawbacks of technology in English today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use the vocabulary (e.g., 'benefit,' 'problem,' 'solution') and grammar (e.g., 'should,' 'can') from today to talk about technology with my family or friends?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group discussions were generally engaging, and students actively used "should" and "can" for suggestions. However, some students struggled with spontaneous use of specific multisyllabic vocabulary like "integration" or "innovation," relying on simpler terms. The intonation practice for modals needs more reinforcement. I will prepare more targeted pronunciation drills for the next speaking opportunity and create a mini-challenge for vocabulary recall. Providing sentence starters for weighing benefits and drawbacks could also be beneficial for less confident speakers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about the use of technology in school. In Lesson 4, we will transition to Writing Composition. Students will use the vocabulary and grammar practiced today to write a short opinion paragraph about the appropriate use of technology in school, drawing on the ideas and arguments generated during today's discussions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 2 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>